  "actual_usd": 0.000465304,
  "answer": "The qualification is that PDF citation availability is not guaranteed for image-only or low-quality scans; it depends on document quality. Public help-center guidance states that scanned PDFs of low quality may cause the system to struggle to generate page-specific citations due to OCR limitations, so support should recommend checking scan quality or re-saving the document in a digital text-searchable format [NL-028-S03]. More specifically, although PDFs are broadly advertised as supporting precise page/paragraph citations, this capability is highly dependent on document quality: scanned, photocopied, or image-only PDFs lack native text layers and require OCR, and low-quality or misaligned scans can produce broken/incomplete text blocks, leading to missing citations, incorrect page links, or TutorChat saying it cannot find the answer [NL-002-S09]. A low-resolution scanned PDF can also cause “No citation available” because the layout parser cannot determine the exact page or section ID [NL-006-S04]. Therefore, technical support answers must add that image-only and low-quality scans may not yield reliable PDF citations, and should advise converting such documents to native text or digital text-searchable formats before upload [NL-002-S09][NL-028-S03].\n\nANSWER_STATUS: answer\nUSED_MEMORY_CLAIMS: none",
